\begin{theorem}\label{main_thm}
Let $S$ and $\AA \rightarrow S$ be as above and assume that $\AA$ is not isotrivial. Let $\c \subseteq \AA$ an irreducible curve defined over $\Qal$ that is neither contained in a fixed fiber nor in a translate of a proper flat subgroup scheme of $\AA$ by a constant section, even after a finite base change. Then, the intersection of $\c$ with the union of all proper algebraic subgroups of the CM fibers of $\AA$ is a finite set.
\end{theorem}

sketch:
The introduction states that the proof of Theorem~\ref{main_thm} \emph{“follows the well-established Pila-Zannier strategy”}. The outlined steps are:

1. \emph{Reduction/uniformization and definability.} One \emph{“first reduce[s] the problem to the case of restrictions of the universal family of abelian varieties over a quasi-projective curve in the moduli space $\A_g$”}. Then, using Peterzil--Starchenko, \emph{“after restricting to a suitable fundamental domain, the uniformizing map of the universal family is definable in the o-minimal structure $\R_{\text{an, exp}}$”}. Hence \emph{“the preimage of $\mathcal{C}$ under this map is a definable surface $X$.”}

2. \emph{Encoding the exceptional set by integer-coefficient conditions.} Let $\mathcal{C}'$ be the subset of $\mathcal{C}$ to be shown finite. The sketch says: \emph{“each point $P_0 \in \mathcal{C}'$ correspond[s] to a point on $X$ lying on a subvariety defined by equations with integer coefficients.”}

3. \emph{An o-minimal counting upper bound (Habegger--Pila).} One applies \emph{“a result by Habegger and Pila”} giving that the number of points of $X$ lying on such subvarieties with coefficients bounded by $T$ is \emph{“at most $\ll T^{\varepsilon}$”}, assuming \emph{“the abelian logarithm of the generic point of $\mathcal{C}$ generates a field of sufficiently large transcendence degree over the field generated by the period matrix.”}

4. \emph{Producing many points with controlled coefficient size via endomorphisms (Barroero--Capuano/Masser).} Using \emph{“a result by Barroero and Capuano, based on an earlier result by Masser”}, one constructs \emph{“a linear combination of a specific basis of endomorphisms of $\AA_{\pi(P_0)}$, with coefficients bounded by a constant times a positive power of $[\mathbb{Q}(P_0):\mathbb{Q}]$ and such that $P_0$ lies in the kernel of this linear combination.”}

5. \emph{Galois orbit lower bound and comparison to force bounded degree.} Since \emph{“all Galois conjugates of $P_0$ remain in $\mathcal{C}'$”}, there are \emph{“at least $D_0$ points on $X$”} lying on subvarieties whose coefficients are bounded by some positive power of $D_0$ (with $D_0=[\mathbb{Q}(P_0):\mathbb{Q}]$). \emph{“Together with the previous estimate, this implies that $D_0$ is uniformly bounded.”}

6. \emph{Conclusion via Northcott.} \emph{“By Northcott's theorem, this establishes the claim of the theorem.”}

The sketch also highlights a key ingredient needed for constructing the endomorphism: \emph{“an explicit control on the behaviour of canonical heights under endomorphisms.”}

